  "Imp2" [label="(0,0,0) Efficiency comparisons with other strategies indicate a performance gap relative to potential."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates ferroelectric PbTiO3 photocatalysis by testing whether Ti defects on positively polarized facets impede photogenerated-electron transfer for hydrogen evolution, and evaluates selective overgrowth of defect-free SrTiO3 nanolayers as surface-defect control to construct an efficient electron pathway that raises the apparent quantum efficiency for overall water splitting at 365 nm, thereby motivating precise surface-defect control as a route to high-performance ferroelectric photocatalysis.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Across the retained and repaired conclusions, ferroelectric photocatalysis is framed as promising because of polarization-driven charge separation but limited by surface charge-transfer barriers that must be identified and controlled."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectric materials have attracted attention because symmetry-breaking electrostatic boundary conditions generate a depolarization field that gives them distinctive physical properties."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) To improve ferroelectric photocatalysis, it is important to identify what limits transfer of photogenerated charges to surface reaction sites rather than focusing only on bulk charge separation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The study is motivated to examine factors that limit transfer of photogenerated charges to surface reaction sites in ferroelectric photocatalytic water splitting."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) A charge-transfer-focused investigation can address the question of which factors restrict photogenerated charges from reaching surface reaction sites in ferroelectric photocatalysis."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Because ferroelectric solar-energy conversion is both promising and practically challenging, specific strategies for improving water-splitting performance require direct empirical evaluation."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) One major category of ferroelectric research focuses on polarization variation, including applications such as ferroelectric memories, negative capacitance, and the electrocaloric effect."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because ferroelectrics possess polarization-related properties that motivate a major research category, their polarization behavior provides a basis for studying functional applications, without implying a specific Schottky-barrier or cocatalyst mechanism."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectric PbTiO3 can support polarization-driven separation of photogenerated carriers, with holes and electrons moving in opposite polarization directions and increased polarization-direction thickness enhancing the charge-separation driving force."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Solar-energy conversion using ferroelectrics is a promising but practically challenging application area."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Although ferroelectric PbTiO3 has polarization-driven charge-separation capability, reported ferroelectric photocatalytic efficiencies remain lower than those achieved by several other strategies, indicating an unresolved performance gap."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectrics differ from conventional semiconductors in that carrier migration can be driven by the depolarization field, and PbTiO3 provides a concrete example in which polarization-direction thickness enhances the charge-separation driving force."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The coexistence of polarization-driven charge separation with lower reported ferroelectric photocatalytic efficiencies suggests that strong internal charge separation alone does not guarantee high water-splitting performance."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Since the discovery of ferroelectrics in Rochelle salt in the 1920s, ferroelectric materials have garnered considerable attention due to their unique structural properties."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) In fact, the efficiencies reported so far for ferroelectrics are significantly lower compared to other strategies, such as p-n junctions, heterojunctions, facet engineering, defect introduction and doping methods, cocatalysts, the Dember effect, and Z-scheme systems."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) This raises an important question: what are the key factors limiting the transfer of photogenerated charges to surface reaction sites?"];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) The symmetry-breaking nature of the electrostatic boundary condition generates a permanent depolarization field confers ferroelectrics with unique physical properties."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Presently, studies on ferroelectrics mainly fall into two categories."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) One focuses on polarization variation, such as ferroelectric memories, negative capacitance, and the electrocaloric effect."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) The other explores the coupling between ferroelectric polarization and light, such as the electro-optic effect, bulk photovoltaic effect, and photoelectric or photocatalytic applications."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Despite significant efforts in both areas, the practical application of ferroelectrics still faces significant challenges."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) One of the most promising and challenging applications of ferroelectrics is the conversion of solar energy into electricity or chemicals, facilitated by the intrinsic advantages of asymmetrical unit cells for charge separation and transfer."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Distinct from other semiconductors, where carrier migration primarily occurs through drift and diffusion, ferroelectrics exhibit a unique mechanism: before screening, carriers predominantly migrate by drifting within the depolarization electric field."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) Specifically, positive holes and negative electrons move in opposite polarization directions, and increasing the thickness along the polarization direction of ferroelectric PbTiO3 enhances the driving force for charge separation."];
